---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const DEMO_URL = "https://gen-ui.jeffmills.dev";
const SOURCE_URL = "https://github.com/k0bura/gen-ui-demo";
---

<Base
  title="Gen UI Demo, Jeff Mills"
  description="A Claude tool-use pipeline that fetches live data, composes UI from a web-component library, and enforces a design system's layout rules in code, without generating any code."
>
  <p class="project-back"><a href="/projects">&laquo; all projects</a></p>
  <h1 class="page-title">gen ui demo</h1>
  <p class="project-meta">
    <span class="project-year">2026</span>
    <span class="meta-sep">&middot;</span>
    <span class="project-status">live</span>
    <span class="meta-sep">&middot;</span>
    <span>v0.4</span>
    <span class="meta-sep">&middot;</span>
    <span>solo</span>
  </p>

  <p class="page-lede">
    Type a question. A chain of Claude tool-use calls fetches live data,
    picks components from a framework-agnostic web-component library,
    gives the content structure, and arranges it into a layout. Plain code
    runs between the model calls and enforces the design rules. The
    renderer is plain Svelte. The LLM never writes code.
  </p>

  <aside class="callout">
    <span class="callout-label">The constraint</span>
    <p>
      When I built the original at Vanguard, AI-generated code was off the
      table for production. So the question became: can an LLM compose a UI
      from a known set of trusted components, without writing the code that
      renders them? If yes, the LLM stays in its safe lane (content
      generation, structured selection) and the application code keeps its
      deterministic guarantees.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <aside class="callout">
    <span class="callout-label">What changed in v0.4</span>
    <p>
      The first version proved the idea in two days: two model calls, one
      to pick components and one to lay them out. Taking the same idea
      further at work taught me where a pipeline like that falls short of
      something you would put in front of a user. This version is a
      from-scratch public rebuild of those lessons: real data instead of
      model recall, a design system's layout rules written down as data,
      and deterministic code that holds those rules when a prompt is
      ignored.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <aside class="callout">
    <span class="callout-label">Why Shoelace and Carbon</span>
    <p>
      Shoelace is the public stand-in for Meridian. Both are
      framework-agnostic web-component libraries with declarative props,
      named slots, and a <code>custom-elements.json</code> manifest
      describing every component. The component tools the model can call
      are generated from that manifest at build time. The layout rules
      come from a second public source: IBM Carbon's grid, spacing scale,
      and type tokens, which are documented openly and ship in a free
      Figma Community kit. I evaluated moving the components to Carbon's
      web components too, and kept Shoelace because its manifest is the
      one rich enough to generate tool schemas from: resolved enums,
      documented slots, real descriptions.
    </p>
  </aside>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <div class="cta-row">
    <a class="cta-primary" href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Open the live demo &raquo;
    </a>
    <a class="cta-secondary" href={SOURCE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      Source on GitHub &nearr;
    </a>
  </div>
  <p class="cta-note">
    The demo runs as a standalone app at
    <code>{DEMO_URL.replace("https://", "")}</code>. Reviewers can clone
    the repo and <code>npm run dev</code> in under a minute, with a mock
    mode that needs no API key.
  </p>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">how it works</h2>

  <div
    class="diagram"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Pipeline: the user prompt feeds a components stage that calls live data tools and emits components; a normalize pass cleans the markup; a structure stage sets order, groups and text roles; a layout stage picks row patterns; code repairs the layout and sets type; a Svelte renderer draws the final UI."
  >
    <div class="diagram-node">user prompt</div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">&#9660;</div>
    <div class="diagram-node is-stage">
      <div class="diagram-node-title">components (LLM loop)</div>
      <div class="diagram-node-body">call live data tools<br />+ emit components</div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">
      &#9660;<br /><span class="diagram-payload">normalize (code)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-node is-stage">
      <div class="diagram-node-title">structure + roles (LLM)</div>
      <div class="diagram-node-body">order, groups, text roles<br />never sizes</div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">
      &#9660;<br /><span class="diagram-payload">one summary line per component</span>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-node is-stage">
      <div class="diagram-node-title">layout (cheaper LLM)</div>
      <div class="diagram-node-body">pick from six row patterns</div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">
      &#9660;<br /><span class="diagram-payload">repair + typeset (code)</span>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-node">
      <div class="diagram-node-title">svelte renderer</div>
      <div class="diagram-node-body">deterministic</div>
    </div>
    <div class="diagram-arrow">&#9660;</div>
    <div class="diagram-node">final UI + follow-up prompts</div>
  </div>

  <dl class="stage-list">
    <dt>Stage 1, data and components</dt>
    <dd>
      The only agentic stage. The model gets one flat list of tools of two
      kinds. Data tools are executed: keyless public APIs for weather,
      exchange rates, and encyclopedia summaries. Component tools are never
      executed. The call is the output: the app records the tool input as a
      component instance and replies "rendered" so the model carries on.
      Around thirty component tools are generated from the Shoelace
      manifest, plus hand-written chart and table tools. The first round
      must call a tool, so the model cannot answer in prose.
    </dd>

    <dt>Stage 2, structure and roles</dt>
    <dd>
      A second model reorders and groups the components and marks a role on
      every piece of text: card title, headline figure, body, caption. It
      never chooses a size. If its answer is missing or malformed, stage
      1's list passes through unchanged, and a rewrite that introduces a
      number stage 1 did not produce is rejected for that component.
    </dd>

    <dt>Stage 3, layout</dt>
    <dd>
      A cheaper model never sees the markup. It gets one summary line per
      component (id, tag, group, a density summary, and a minimum width the
      code computed) and must call a single layout tool whose pattern enum
      is generated from the layout spec. A small, structural task is why
      the cheaper model is enough.
    </dd>

    <dt>Code passes, between and after</dt>
    <dd>
      Normalization strips inline styles, invented classes, scripts, and
      images that did not come from a tool result. Layout repair guarantees
      every component appears exactly once, rows use one of the closed
      patterns, nothing sits in a slot narrower than its minimum, and a
      group stays together. Typesetting turns each role into a type style
      and steps down a size ladder until the text fits its slot, with one
      size shared across a group so cards in a row match.
    </dd>

    <dt>Follow-ups and memory</dt>
    <dd>
      A small final call suggests three next prompts and can never fail the
      request. The server is stateless: the browser sends back each earlier
      prompt with a short note of what was shown, and every turn fetches
      its data again instead of trusting numbers from an earlier one.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">the layout spec: one file, four consumers</h2>

  <p>
    A design system's layout rules usually live in a Figma file and in
    designers' heads. To get a model to follow them you write them into a
    prompt, and to guarantee them you write them again as code. Two copies
    drift.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here the rules are data. One spec file holds the grid, a closed set of
    six row patterns, the narrowest slot each kind of component may
    occupy, the grouping rules, and a table mapping text roles to type
    styles with a size ladder for each. Four things are generated from it:
    the prompt text, the layout tool's schema, the stylesheet, and the
    validators the repair pass runs.
  </p>

  <pre class="prompt-excerpt"><code>{`rowPatterns: [
  { id: "1-up",             tracks: [1] },
  { id: "2-up",             tracks: [1, 1] },
  { id: "2-up-wide-left",   tracks: [2, 1] },
  { id: "2-up-wide-right",  tracks: [1, 2] },
  { id: "3-up",             tracks: [1, 1, 1] },
  { id: "4-up",             tracks: [1, 1, 1, 1] },
]`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Change a number in the spec and the prompt and the enforcement change
    together. The model is asked for the pattern, and the code guarantees
    it. That split is the main thing I would carry into any production
    version: a prompt is a request, and anything that must hold needs to
    be checked by something that cannot be talked out of it.
  </p>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">roles, not sizes</h2>

  <p>
    Asking a model to pick font sizes produces pages where every card
    makes its own decision. The model is good at knowing that a string is
    a headline figure and bad at knowing whether it fits in a 240px slot.
    So the model labels meaning and code does the fitting.
  </p>

  <pre class="prompt-excerpt"><code>{`<span data-role="stat-value">1 USD = 0.92 EUR</span>
<span data-role="caption">as of today</span>`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The typesetting pass computes each component's slot width from the
    repaired layout, estimates the text's width at each step of that
    role's ladder, and takes the largest size that fits. Three figures in
    one row step down together, so the row reads as a set.
  </p>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">data tools shaped for a model</h2>

  <ul class="proof-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Small, flat, labelled, pre-rounded.</strong> A tool returns
      what the page needs, not the raw API response. Arithmetic happens in
      code, so the model does none.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Honest failure.</strong> An error goes back to the model as
      data, with an instruction to say the data could not be retrieved and
      not to fill the gap from memory. A tool that throws never fails the
      run. Ask for the weather in a place that does not exist and you get a
      warning on the page and everything else intact.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Caveats travel with the data.</strong> A result can carry a
      note the model is told to pass on when it changes how a figure
      should be read.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bounded latency.</strong> Every call has an abort timeout and
      a short cache, because one prompt often asks about the same thing
      twice.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">tested where it can be</h2>

  <p>
    Model output is not unit-testable. Everything around it is. The
    normalize, repair, and typeset passes are pure functions, and the
    orchestrator takes the model and the network as arguments, so the
    whole pipeline runs under test with a scripted model. The suite has a
    little over a hundred tests, including a property-style test that
    feeds the repair pass hundreds of random layout trees and checks that
    what comes out always validates against the spec. The page also has a
    "what the pipeline did" panel that shows each repair the code made on
    a live run.
  </p>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">what this proved</h2>
  <ul class="proof-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The composition / rendering split holds.</strong> The LLM
      reasons about which components and what content; the application code
      draws. Each side is good at its job.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tool schemas are the contract.</strong> Constraining all model
      output to typed JSON eliminates the code-correctness and injection
      risks that block production use of free-form LLM output.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The manifest is the API surface.</strong> Because the
      component tools are generated from <code>custom-elements.json</code>
      at build time, the design system stays the source of truth. The
      quality of that manifest decides whether this works at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Design rules belong in data, enforced in code.</strong> A
      prompt gets you most of the way. The repair and typesetting passes
      are what make the layout dependable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Each stage needs only what its job requires.</strong> The
      layout model sees summaries, not markup. The structure model sees
      components, not history. Smaller inputs let cheaper models do the
      work and make each stage easier to reason about.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A well-designed design system is the substrate.</strong>
      Framework-agnostic web components with constrained props were the
      right shape for this years before generative UI was a real category.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="section-title">known gaps</h2>
  <ul class="proof-list">
    <li>
      <strong>No streaming.</strong> A prompt waits for several sequential
      model calls, and the progress labels are timers.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The sanitizer is an allowlist scanner, not a parser.</strong>
      It is enough for a demo that renders its own model's output. It is
      not what I would ship in front of untrusted input.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sizes are fitted to one canvas width</strong> and do not
      re-fit on resize.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <h2 class="section-title">stack</h2>
  <dl class="stack-list">
    <dt>Frontend</dt>
    <dd>
      Astro, Svelte 5 (runes), Shoelace 2.x web components, Chart.js,
      Tailwind v4 for tokens
    </dd>
    <dt>Pipeline</dt>
    <dd>
      Anthropic Messages API, multi-stage tool use with a tool-calling
      loop, prompt caching on tools, system prompt, and loop rounds
    </dd>
    <dt>Layout rules</dt>
    <dd>
      One spec file based on IBM Carbon's grid, spacing, and type tokens,
      generating prompt text, tool schema, CSS, and validators
    </dd>
    <dt>Data</dt>
    <dd>Keyless public APIs for weather, exchange rates, and encyclopedia summaries</dd>
    <dt>Tool generation</dt>
    <dd>
      Build-time parser over Shoelace&rsquo;s <code>custom-elements.json</code>
      manifest, emitting one Anthropic tool spec per component
    </dd>
    <dt>Tests</dt>
    <dd>Vitest over the deterministic passes and the full pipeline with a scripted model</dd>
    <dt>Hosting</dt>
    <dd>Cloudflare Pages + Pages Functions, key in Pages env vars</dd>
  </dl>

  <hr class="jm-rule" />

  <p class="project-foot">
    Write-ups: <a href="/writing/gen-ui-with-claude-tool-use">Generating UI Without Generating Code</a>
    &middot; <a href={DEMO_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">live demo</a>
    &middot; <a href={SOURCE_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a>
  </p>
</Base>

